\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Blocking a single-site weight}
\date{2026-11-28}

\p{Let #{Q=\sum_{j=0}^k c_jP_j>0}, #{k\ge1}, be a
\vocabk{hierarchical precision}{fdip-01CJ} on the real counting
inner-product space #{\RR^{X_k}}. Let #{\lambda>0},
#{r=\sqrt{p\lambda}}, and let #{V:\RR\to\RR}
be finite Borel and bounded below. Give the Gaussian probability
of precision #{Q} the weight
#{\prod_{x\in X_k}\ue^{-V(\phi(x))}}, and normalize it.
Its pushforward by #{T=rA} is the Gaussian probability of precision
#{\Phi_\lambda(Q)} weighted and normalized by
#{\prod_{b\in X_{k-1}}\ue^{-F_V(y(b))}}, where
##{F_V(y)=-\log\mathbb E_\zeta
\exp\left[-\sum_{t=0}^{p-1}V(y/r+\zeta_t)\right].}
Here #{\zeta} is the centered Gaussian on the zero-sum subspace
of #{\RR^p}, with covariance operator
#{c_0^{-1}(\bbI -E)}, where #{E} is the orthogonal projection onto
constant vectors. The function #{F_V} is finite Borel and bounded
below. If #{V} is continuous, so is #{F_V}. Additive constants in
#{F_V} do not change the normalized probability.}

\proof{
\p{The orthogonal splitting into #{\im E_1} and #{\ker E_1}
commutes with #{Q}. The two Gaussian components are independent,
and the second has covariance #{c_0^{-1}P_0}; this is the
hierarchical case of \ref{fdip-01CH}. Its summands on different
blocks are independent and each has the stated covariance operator.
Conditioned on #{T\phi=y}, the retained fine field is #{Sy/r}.
Thus the conditional expectation of the product weight factors
as #{\prod_b\ue^{-F_V(y(b))}}. The marginal Gaussian has
precision #{\Phi_\lambda(Q)} by \ref{fdip-01CH}. Multiplying its
density by that conditional expectation and dividing by its integral
proves the assertion, by the same Gaussian disintegration as
\ref{fdip-00GI}.}

\p{If #{V\ge -b}, the integrand is strictly positive and at most
#{\ue^{pb}}. Its expectation is positive and finite at every #{y},
so #{F_V\ge -pb}. Parameter integration of a nonnegative Borel
function is Borel. For continuous #{V}, the same constant bounds
the integrand while #{y} varies, and dominated convergence proves
continuity of the expectation and then of its logarithm.
The normalizer is positive and finite. Consequently the same
operation can be repeated with #{F_V}, as in \ref{fdip-00GK}.}
}
